General Motors Company (GMC), a subsidiary of General Motors (GM), is one of the most trusted SUV and pickup truck brands in the world. However, even reliable automakers occasionally need to issue recalls to maintain safety standards.

Recently, several GMC models have been flagged for safety concerns. For owners, it’s important to stay informed about these GMC truck recalls and protect themselves on the road.

At CLINE APC, our [lemon law lawyers in California](/) have helped countless clients preserve their consumer rights. We also take it upon ourselves to keep you informed about important recalls, so you know when and how to act.

In this post, we’ll break down the biggest GMC truck recalls in recent years**,** what they involve, and what you need to do if your vehicle is impacted.

## Recently Recalled GMC Vehicles

Although recalls can often seem concerning, they’re actually a positive sign of accountability from the automaker. Recalls show that the company is taking action to fix potential safety issues and keep drivers safe.

If your GMC vehicle is part of a recall, you can have the issue fixed for free. However, if the problem persists after repairs, you may be eligible to file a claim for a refund or replacement under [California’s lemon law](/info/everything-you-need-to-know-about-california-lemon-law-claims).

With that said, let’s take a closer look at some of the most recent GMC truck recalls and the specific safety concerns that prompted them.

## 1. 2024 GMC Sierra and 2024 GMC Yukon: Brake Fluid Leak Detection Issue

## Models Affected:

- 2023 GMC Sierra 1500

- 2023 GMC Yukon

- 2024 GMC Yukon

- 2023 GMC Yukon XL

- 2024 GMC Yukon XL

## Brake Fluid Detection Failure:

The GMC Sierra 1500, along with several 2023-2024 GMC Yukon models, have been identified with an [issue in their brake fluid leak detection system](https://gmauthority.com/blog/2024/09/gmc-sierra-and-yukon-recalled-for-failing-brake-fluid-loss-detection/#google_vignette). In simple terms, if there’s a brake fluid leak, the system fails to provide any warning.

Without this warning, you wouldn’t know if the brake fluid drops too low, which can lead to reduced brake performance. This means your brake pedal could feel sluggish or less responsive, increasing the risk of accidents or collisions.

If you own any of the affected models, contact GM customer service or visit your dealership to schedule a free repair.

## 2024 GMC Canyon: Headlamp Flicker Issue

## Models Affected:

- 2024 GMC Canyon

## Sudden Headlamp Flicker:

GMC has recalled [13,200 units of the 2024 GMC Canyon](https://gmauthority.com/blog/2024/09/gm-issues-stop-sale-order-on-some-2024-gmc-canyon-pickups/) due to a headlamp flicker that goes against [Federal Motor Vehicle Safety Standard 108](https://www.federalregister.gov/documents/2007/12/04/07-5644/federal-motor-vehicle-safety-standards-lamps-reflective-devices-and-associated-equipment). This is obviously a very serious safety issue, as a faulty headlamp can impact visibility, especially when driving at night or in bad weather.

Currently, about 40% of these trucks are still at dealerships or in transit, with a stop-sale order in place until the issue is resolved.

Owners of 2024 GMC Canyons should look out for communication from GM or visit their website to see if their truck is impacted. If the issue persists after repair, you may be eligible for a refund or buyback under [GMC lemon law](/manufacturers/gmc)**.**

## 3. 2024 GMC Canyon: Fuel Tank Lock Ring Issue

## Models Affected:

- 2024 GMC Canyon

## Unsecured Fuel Tank Lock Ring:

In a separate recall, the 2024 GMC Canyon was [flagged for a loose fuel tank lock ring](https://static.nhtsa.gov/odi/rcl/2024/RCLRPT-24V491-8985.PDF). An improperly sealed fuel tank can leak gasoline during a crash or impact, posing a potential fire hazard.

This GMC truck recall affects 2,492 units of the GMC Canyon and 6,130 units of the Chevy Colorado. While both models share some mechanical features, it’s important to note that the headlamp flicker recall is specific to the GMC Canyon, whereas the fuel tank lock ring issue impacts both vehicles.

## 4. 2020-2024 GMC Sierra 2500/3500: Tailgate Release Issue

## Models Affected:

- 2020-2024 GMC Sierra 2500

- 2020-2024 GMC Sierra 3500

## Faulty Power-Lock and Release System in the Tailgate:

Earlier this year, GM [recalled over 300,000](https://www.usatoday.com/story/money/cars/recalls/2024/02/06/gmc-sierra-silverado-recall/72411204007/) 2020-2024 GMC Sierra 2500/3500 (and Chevy Silverado 2500/3500) trucks due to an issue with the electric tailgate release system caused by water intrusion. Put simply, water can get into the system, short-circuit it, and cause the gate to unlatch by itself - even when the vehicle is in park.

From September 2020 to August 2023, General Motors received numerous complaints of the “unwanted tailgate opening”, with some cases involving minor property damage and injuries. After a thorough investigation, the company officially issued a safety recall to address the defect.

## 5. 2024 GMC Terrain: Defective Door Strikers

## Models Affected:

- 2024 GMC Terrain

## Malfunctioning Door Strikers:

Issued in December 2023, this is one of the smallerGMC truck recalls made recently - affecting only [62 2024 GMC Terrain units](https://gmauthority.com/blog/2023/12/chevy-equinox-blazer-blazer-ev-and-gmc-terrain-recalled-for-fracturing-door-striker/), among others. The recall was due to defective door strikers that didn’t meet GM’s hardness standards.

Reports suggest the door strikers were improperly heat-treated, which made them susceptible to fracture under stress. A broken door striker can cause the door to open unexpectedly while driving or in an accident, significantly increasing the risk of injury to passengers.

As a precaution, dealers have been instructed to replace all four door strikers on affected GMC Terrain vehicles. The GM Warranty Parts Center is providing a kit that includes four-door strikers and the necessary bolts to secure them.

## What to Do If Your GMC Vehicle is Under Recall?

## How to Check

The simplest way to check for a recall on your GMC is by using your Vehicle Identification Number (VIN). Your VIN is a unique 17-character code typically found on the dashboard, near the windshield, or on your vehicle registration paperwork.

You can use this number to search for any open recalls through:

- GM’s official website recall page

- National Highway Traffic Safety Administration (NHTSA) recall lookup tool

You can also visit or call your local GMC dealership, and have them run a VIN check for you.

## Steps to Take

Once you confirm your vehicle is part of the GMC truck recall, you can:

- **Contact Your Dealer:** Reach out to a GMC dealership to schedule a repair. Recalls are fixed at no cost to you.

- **Arrange for Transportation:** If the repair takes time, ask your dealer about getting a loaner vehicle or transportation alternatives so you can keep up with your daily routine.

- **Keep Documentation:** After the repair is completed, make sure to hold onto the paperwork. This documentation is important if the issue resurfaces or if you decide to pursue a lemon law claim later.

## Know Your Rights as a GMC Owner in California

As a GMC owner in California, you have important protections under the state’s lemon law. CA lemon laws safeguard consumers from defective vehicles that can’t be repaired.

Your vehicle may qualify as a lemon if:

- It has a substantial defect affecting its safety, value, or use.

- The manufacturer or dealer has made multiple failed repair attempts (typically two or more). If it’s a serious safety issue (like brake failure), even one repair attempt might be enough.

- Your vehicle has been out of service for 30 or more days (cumulative) due to repairs.

- The defect is not due to owner misuse, neglect, or unauthorized modifications.

If your vehicle is deemed a lemon, you are entitled to several forms of compensation, including:

- A full refund of your initial purchase price

- Reimbursement for incidental damages (down payment, loan payments, registration fees, and other costs)

- A replacement vehicle of the same or similar value

While GMC lemon law is consumer-friendly, you’ll still want to have legal counsel in your corner. A skilled California lemon law lawyer will defend your rights, negotiate effectively, and handle all the heavy-lifting on your behalf.

Better yet, lemon law attorneys cost you nothing upfront, as their fees are included in the manufacturer’s final settlement.

